I'd take a more moderate and/or pessimistic stand about whether all
government services will be replaced in the free market--there are
probably some services(?) that no one will bother to supply.

For example, drug testing might well be less extreme 
in a free market if people decide that the last degree of certainty
isn't worth the cost. 

Also, I believe (though I don't think I can prove it) that governments
won't be more moral than their populace is on the average, though they
may well be less moral. This means that if very few people care about
meticulous drug testing, for example, then even if there's a law 
mandating drug testing it won't be done meticulously--there won't
be enough people who care about doing the job well to staff the
agency, and even if the minority of people who care are in the jobs
the funding they're offered will be insufficient and the rules
they work under will be inappropriate.

