This message contains 4 parts:
1. Observations and questions about Julian Simon
2. A short response to Dave Krieger on whether there's lots of empty land
3. Excerpts from all articles in Science News in 1994 relating to the
proposition that man is greatly damaging the environment (long).
4. A postscript on negative effects of population density.

-- Phil Goetz, goetz@cs.buffalo.edu

Nick Szabo, Max More, Craig Presson, and Dave Krieger all told me
to read Julian Simon.  I would expect to have been referred to a
different source by each person.  Instead, it appears that the
underpopulation dogma rests solely on the work of one man, Julian Simon.
This is suspicious.

We have two choices:

1. Almost everyone examining population growth is wrong, and this ONE MAN,
Julian Simon, has the wisdom to see the Truth.

2. There are a lot of people who want to believe that we are not in
danger of overpopulation, and so they'll prejudice their critical
faculties in favor of any arguments in that direction.  I respect
Nick, Max, and Craig too much to think they could be taken in by
foolishness, but I suspect there is a flaw somewhere they have not seen.

I haven't read Simon, but would like to know if he commits any of
these errors:

1. He expects that individuals in the future will continue to live using
roughly the same amount of resources as individuals in the present.
The idea that people can have "enough" energy is flawed -- as the price
goes down, we will find new uses for energy.  One frightening trend is
the development of fat-free, calorie-free foods.  It may soon be possible
for one person to eat as much as three people could previously, without
becoming fat.  How does that affect his predictions of food supply?

2. When he speaks of how many people the Earth can support, I suspect
he is assuming that all resources will be devoted to supporting humans.
In other words, land that is wild is wasted -- energy is being used to
support activity other than human living.  Some extropians agree with this,
as I've learned (not to my surprise) from the poll answers.  Sometime
soon I will compose a lengthy message explaining why I think this is
based on an underlying morality which, while not inconsistent, will lead
to an undesirable transhumanist environment lacking diversity and full
of strife.

3. The error of believing that human societies can distribute resources
optimally.  There will always be large inefficiencies in a complex social
or economic system.

4. Data selection:  The classic example of this is Creationists who
argue that carbon-dating is a hoax because someone, somewhere, dated
something that was alive and the process indicated an age of 20,000 years.
They will select 2 or 3 incidents, ignoring thousands that contradict
their beliefs.


